{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Cursed be the man.</b> Now all the curses were first stated in the language of blessing, as is received in the tradition; therefore it says: <b>These shall stand to bless the people</b> (Deuteronomy 27:12). However, Scripture mentions them only in the language of 'cursed,' because the principal intent in these curses was to curse those who transgress these things, so that they alone bear their iniquity and the rest of the people are not held surety for them. This is because those who commit these sins were for the most part the leaders of the people, whom the common folk had no power to restrain, as Ezekiel testified, saying: <b>The princes of Israel, each according to his arm, have been in you to shed blood; father and mother they have despised in you</b> (Ezekiel 22:6–7). And in that same passage the prophet mentioned most or all of these curses, and said that these sins were committed in Jerusalem — not that the entire city sinned in this, but rather the leaders sinned in such things. Yet when he mentioned there the sins of the public, he held the city accountable, saying: <b>You have despised my holy things and profaned my sabbaths</b> (Ezekiel 22:8)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:22:23.500588883Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}